

We really tried to replicate the system environment by having roles (Admin, Team Lead, Specialist)
Where the admin creates a ticket
The Team lead assigns it
The Specialist solves it

There is also an overview of the business entities and there current workload

In practice:

A Ticket arrives, the admin enters the title and description. Nothing more.
Business Entity, priority, impact, urgency, a can be managed either through AI, or manually
When managed through AI, there is the possibility to use several LLMs including Apertus,
which use context extraction from the description the admin entered.

We have optimized the AI functionalities in a way that the priority matrix defines a priority
score between 0 and 1 through mathematical formulas. They give weights
to attributes like "age of the ticket", whether several entities are impacted by the ticket,
whether there is a regulatory breach, etc. They are fully documented in the project description.
Reason why we focused on these mathematical formulas was to avoid LLM hallucinations.

Once a ticket is entered in the system, the ticket goes to a Team Lead (Business entity IT lead)
which gets recommendation metrics from our system, and decides to which specialist of the business
entity the ticket should be assigned to.

The admin, the Team Leads and the specialists have the possibility to see at any time the status
of the system and most importantly the workload of the specialists.

We didn't want the AI to by-pass the role of the team-lead according to the description of the case which
clearly mentions "The team-lead remains in control and approves, edits or rejects the recommendation"

An specialist receives a ticket, he can either reject it because he thinks it's not for him,
accept it or escalate it if the ticket is lacking information.

He can escalate the ticket either to the team-lead or to the entire team.

Once the ticket is approved, it is assumed that the specialist will solve the ticket and once solved,
mark the ticket as solved. This triggers 2 actions: The ticket a resolution text auto-generated by AI.
This is very important, because we all know that once having solved a complex issue, people are lazy
and avoiding to write a detailed resolution. However, its obvious that the specialist can modify this
AI generated text.






Problem: Efficiency of the ticket system allocation and resolution in terms of time and quality
Solution: Collaborative environment with AI assistance which enhances transparency, explainability
and efficiency
Concretely:
Training based on 20k tickets
Time to treat a ticket reduced

AI-recommended ticket allocation based on workload and relevance of service teams
with respect to people
We hosted the entire app and the link can be found in Github. The whole process is documented
Some performance metrics are available to measure the impact of our app in comparison to
existing approach


